1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:04,456
Here's the whole plate, top to bottom —
nothing hidden, nothing cut away.

2
00:00:04,456 --> 00:00:07,639
A plate layout mistake never announces
itself.

3
00:00:07,639 --> 00:00:11,859
You find it a week later, in the data —
after the reagent is gone, and the last

4
00:00:11,859 --> 00:00:15,544
aliquot of an irreplaceable sample is
sitting in the wrong well.

5
00:00:15,544 --> 00:00:16,586
This is the job.

6
00:00:16,586 --> 00:00:19,201
Ninety-six near-identical wells.

7
00:00:19,201 --> 00:00:21,273
Controls locked in the corners.

8
00:00:21,273 --> 00:00:23,461
Replicates that have to stay apart.

9
00:00:23,461 --> 00:00:25,185
Edge wells that evaporate.

10
00:00:25,185 --> 00:00:29,236
Tedious, spatial work that people are bad
at and agents should be good at.

11
00:00:29,236 --> 00:00:31,354
But not the way agents usually work.

12
00:00:31,354 --> 00:00:36,088
A chat assistant can describe a beautiful
layout; it can't put one on your plate.

13
00:00:36,088 --> 00:00:38,187
An agent driving your screen sees

14
00:00:38,187 --> 00:00:43,359
ninety-six identical boxes — coordinates
never say which well holds your scarce

15
00:00:43,359 --> 00:00:47,106
sample, or whether its plan went stale
thirty seconds ago.

16
00:00:47,106 --> 00:00:49,722
So PlateWeave gives the page its own
voice.

17
00:00:49,722 --> 00:00:51,804
Nine WebMCP tools expose the same

18
00:00:51,804 --> 00:00:56,785
versioned state the scientist sees —
sample identities, locks, constraints,

19
00:00:56,785 --> 00:01:01,840
computed metrics — so the agent reasons
about the experiment instead of pixels.

20
00:01:01,840 --> 00:01:05,428
Then I ask for what a scientist actually
wants, in one sentence.

21
00:01:05,428 --> 00:01:07,961
Keep the controls fixed, samples off the

22
00:01:07,961 --> 00:01:11,238
edge, replicates apart, and show me the
trade-off.

23
00:01:11,238 --> 00:01:15,971
Codex generates two deterministic
candidates — one tuned for statistical

24
00:01:15,971 --> 00:01:20,556
balance, one for pipetting speed — and
asks the page to score its own work.

25
00:01:20,556 --> 00:01:22,981
The recommendation lands as a reversible

26
00:01:22,981 --> 00:01:25,752
preview, and the whole layout changes at
once.

27
00:01:25,752 --> 00:01:27,292
Now the part that matters.

28
00:01:27,292 --> 00:01:31,400
This sample is scarce, so I place it at D6
and lock it myself.

29
00:01:31,400 --> 00:01:33,958
That edit bumps the state version, making

30
00:01:33,958 --> 00:01:37,465
the agent's earlier plan stale — and the
page refuses it.

31
00:01:37,465 --> 00:01:40,405
Codex rebuilds around my lock and
validates.

32
00:01:40,405 --> 00:01:41,875
My well never moves.

33
00:01:41,875 --> 00:01:43,843
Export is a harder boundary.

34
00:01:43,843 --> 00:01:46,351
The agent can prepare the exact file and

35
00:01:46,351 --> 00:01:51,215
its hash, but when it tries to export, the
page answers: approval required.

36
00:01:51,215 --> 00:01:52,616
That isn't the model's call.

37
00:01:52,616 --> 00:01:55,342
I approve the hash myself, and one export

38
00:01:55,342 --> 00:01:59,271
goes through — bound to that exact layout,
with a receipt.

39
00:01:59,271 --> 00:02:02,546
Ask again, and you get the same receipt,
never a second file.

40
00:02:02,546 --> 00:02:05,486
That's the shape of trustworthy agent work
on the web.

41
00:02:05,486 --> 00:02:07,770
The page keeps the rules, the agent does

42
00:02:07,770 --> 00:02:11,991
the precision, and the scientist keeps the
judgment and the last click.

43
00:02:11,991 --> 00:02:14,076
Nobody clicked ninety-six times, and

44
00:02:14,076 --> 00:02:17,465
nothing that mattered happened without a
person saying yes.
